# Visualizing Data with Tables

While conducting research, numbers, responses, and overall data will be collected into one database.

It will be the duty of the researcher to analyze the raw data and determine what parts and combinations of the information will be useful in answering the hypothesis at hand.

If data is left unfiltered it becomes challenging in order to provide quantitative evidence of the solution needed.

## *gt* and *gtExtra*

- Excellent packages in R studio that allow accessible table and data analysis

- *gt* is the ability to create tables, structure how tables are set up, and directly works with the data provided by the researcher

- *gtExtra* allows for that polished table to read clearly as well as set up themes and more variety of options to showcase the tables in a coherent want

## Tables vs. Charts

Both are incredible yet different ways of showcasing data and findings in a report or presentation.

Together that are not competing tools, but complete different jobs in order to create fuller picture of the date being presented.

> Charts
>
> - Fantastic for showcasing patterns and trends
>
> - Comparing variables without exact numbers
>
> - Showcase relationships between correlation and distribution (X and Y axis)

> Tables
>
> - Showcase exactly, precise values
>
> - Displays a larger amount of data in rows and columns that is not visually cluttered
>
> - Tables are searchable and researchers can analyze specific rows and columns with tools
>
> - Quantitative and Qualitative data can be recorded into tables for easy access

Although Separately they have their own functions: together they provide a clearer understanding on the data as well as visual representation for both the researcher and the audience to understand.

### Examples With PizzaPlace Data: RAW

Although data will be implemented with AO1; there is currently no data to present at the moment, so I will utilize the Pizza Place Data found in the *gt* package.

```{r}
library(gt)
library(gtExtras)

data("pizzaplace")
head(pizzaplace)

```

> This is a full table with all the information in it. To make it easier to digest; it can be sorted and visualized in many ways.

#### Ex. *Titles/Color*

```{r}
#| message: false
library(gt)
library(gtExtras)
library(dplyr)
library(lubridate)

orders_by_hour <- pizzaplace %>%
  mutate(hour = hour(hms::as_hms(time))) %>%
  group_by(hour) %>%
  summarize(
    orders = n(),
    total_revenue = sum(price),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(orders))   # <-- sorts hours by busiest first

orders_by_hour %>%
  gt(rowname_col = "hour") %>%
  tab_header(
    title = "When Are Pizzas Actually Being Ordered?",
    subtitle = "Hours ranked from busiest to slowest"
  ) %>%
  tab_stubhead(label = "Hour") %>%
  cols_label(
    orders = "Orders Placed",
    total_revenue = "Total Revenue"
  ) %>%
  tab_spanner(
    label = "Order Volume Summary",
    columns = c(orders, total_revenue)
  ) %>%
  fmt_currency(columns = total_revenue, decimals = 2) %>%
  data_color(
    columns = orders,
    fn = scales::col_numeric(palette = c("#dbeafe", "#1e3a8a"), domain = NULL)
  ) %>%
  gt_plt_bar(column = orders, color = "#2563eb", scale_type = "number") %>%
  tab_source_note(source_note = "Source: pizzaplace dataset, gt package.") %>%
  tab_footnote(
    footnote = "Hour extracted from the order timestamp (24-hour format).",
    locations = cells_stubhead()
  ) %>%
  tab_footnote(
    footnote = "Total Revenue reflects the sum of all order prices placed during that hour.",
    locations = cells_column_labels(columns = total_revenue)
  )
```

### Ex. 2: *Organization for Specific Analysis* 

```{r}
#| message: false
library(gt)
library(gtExtras)
library(dplyr)
library(stringr)

top5_pizzas <- pizzaplace %>%
  mutate(
    name = str_replace_all(name, "_", " "),
    name = str_replace_all(name, regex("ckn", ignore_case = TRUE), "Chicken"),
    name = str_replace_all(name, regex("dlx", ignore_case = TRUE), "Delux"),
    name = str_to_title(name)
  ) %>%
  group_by(name) %>%
  summarize(
    orders = n(),
    avg_price = mean(price),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) %>%
  slice_max(orders, n = 5) %>%
  arrange(desc(orders))   # <-- sorted by popularity now, not price

top5_pizzas %>%
  gt(rowname_col = "name") %>%
  tab_header(
    title = "Fan Favorites, Ranked by Popularity",
    subtitle = "The 5 most frequently ordered pizzas, most to least popular"
  ) %>%
  tab_stubhead(label = "Pizza") %>%
  cols_label(
    orders = "Times Ordered",
    avg_price = "Average Price"
  ) %>%
  tab_spanner(
    label = "Popularity & Price",
    columns = c(orders, avg_price)
  ) %>%
  fmt_currency(columns = avg_price, decimals = 2) %>%
  data_color(
    columns = avg_price,
    fn = scales::col_numeric(palette = c("#fef3c7", "#b45309"), domain = NULL)
  ) %>%
  gt_plt_bar(column = orders, color = "#b45309", scale_type = "number") %>%
  tab_source_note(source_note = "Source: pizzaplace dataset, gt package.") %>%
  tab_footnote(
    footnote = "Popularity measured by total number of orders across the full dataset.",
    locations = cells_column_labels(columns = orders)
  ) %>%
  tab_footnote(
    footnote = "Average Price reflects the mean price across all sizes ordered for that pizza.",
    location= cells_column_labels(columns = avg_price)
  )
```

- These tables are able to break down the information with digestible information

- With my particular AO it is important to recognize the impact data and numbers make over time paired with Qualitative responses

- Being able to showcase tables with responses will allow presentations and documents to have the proper evidence to support the hypothesis
